-- Week 3 Build Day (session 7, 2026-08-13) — replace the generated 5-artifact
-- card with a guided, self-contained 7-step lab.
--
-- Why: the project system is not finished, so the lab must not depend on a
-- student having a registered project. Steps 1-6 are a fixed shared example
-- (inbox triage); only the optional step 7 uses {PROJECT}, which the
-- BuildArtifactsRender dropdown substitutes (own repos + 3 sample projects).
--
-- Contract notes (frontend/src/components/timeline/BuildArtifactsRender.tsx):
--   * parseArtifacts() reads only DIRECT children of the body: <h4> starts a
--     step, <p> appends to its blurb, <pre> is the copy-to-clipboard prompt.
--     Exactly one <pre> per step (the last one wins) and no wrapper divs.
--   * The submit uploader ACCEPTs .pdf/.docx/.pptx/.xlsx/.rtf/.txt/.md/.csv —
--     NOT .py. Step 6's deliverable is run_report.md for that reason.
--   * metadata.locked = true stops ensureFreshContent() regenerating over this
--     hand-authored body after the 30-day TTL (cardContentService.ts:184).
--
-- Idempotent: keyed on the card id / session id, safe to re-run.

UPDATE timeline_cards
SET
  title          = $t$Build Your First AI Workflow$t$,
  subtitle       = $t$Seven prompts, one working tool$t$,
  description    = $t$Build a working Python workflow tool with the Claude API: set up your key, triage a whole inbox, and add the quality gate that makes it trustworthy.$t$,
  estimated_time = 60,
  metadata       = jsonb_set(
                     jsonb_set(
                       jsonb_set(metadata, '{locked}', 'true'::jsonb, true),
                       '{content_at}', to_jsonb(now()::text), true
                     ),
                     '{content}',
                     $json$
{
  "title": "Build Day Lab — Your First AI Workflow",
  "summary": "Seven prompts, about an hour. By the end you have a working Python tool that reads a folder of messages, triages every one through the Claude API, and writes a report you could hand to a manager. Work the steps in order using the picker above.",
  "body_html": "<h4>Step 1 · Set up your workspace and your API key</h4>\n<p>Start here. This is the only step where you type something in yourself instead of pasting a prompt. Your API key goes into a file called .env, and it never goes into a chat window, into a script, or into anything you share. Run the prompt below first.</p>\n<p>When it finishes: open the .env file it points you to, replace paste-your-key-here with your key from console.anthropic.com (Settings, then API keys), and save. No quotes, no spaces around the equals sign. Everything after this is copy, paste, run.</p>\n<pre>I am starting a hands-on lab and I am not a programmer. Please explain each step in plain English as you go, and stop to tell me if something needs my attention.\n\nCreate a folder called inbox-triage inside my Downloads folder, and work inside it for everything that follows. In that folder:\n\n1. Check that Python 3 is installed and tell me the version. If it is missing, tell me how to install it for my operating system and stop there.\n2. Install the anthropic and python-dotenv packages.\n3. Create a file named .env containing exactly these two lines:\nANTHROPIC_API_KEY=paste-your-key-here\nANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5\n4. Create a .gitignore file containing .env, so the key can never be committed by accident.\n5. Print the full path to the .env file and tell me how to open it in a text editor on my machine.\n\nDo not ask me for my API key, and do not put a key into any file yourself. I will paste it into .env on my own.</pre>\n\n<h4>Step 2 · Prove the connection works</h4>\n<p>One small script that makes one API call. If this works, everything after it will. If it does not, the error tells us exactly what to fix, and it is almost always a typo in the key or a stray space.</p>\n<p>You should see a one-sentence reply plus a token count. Those numbers are what you are paying for, so they are worth watching from the start.</p>\n<pre>In the inbox-triage folder, create check_setup.py using the official anthropic Python SDK.\n\nIt should load ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and ANTHROPIC_MODEL from the .env file using python-dotenv, send one short message to Claude asking it to reply with a single sentence confirming the connection works, and then print three things: which model answered, the reply, and the input and output token counts from the response usage.\n\nIf the key is missing, empty, or still the placeholder text, print a plain-English message telling me exactly what to fix instead of a stack trace. Do the same for an authentication failure.\n\nThen run it and show me the output.</pre>\n\n<h4>Step 3 · Make a realistic inbox to work on</h4>\n<p>The tool needs something to chew on. Rather than hand you a download, we will have Claude Code generate a believable inbox, so everyone starts from the same place and no real correspondence is involved.</p>\n<pre>In the inbox-triage folder, create a subfolder named inbox and fill it with 12 plain text files, one message per file, named msg01.txt through msg12.txt.\n\nMake them read like a real week in a mid-size company shared inbox: two or three genuinely urgent (a system outage, an angry customer about to cancel), several routine (an invoice question, a meeting reschedule, a vendor checking in), one vague message that is genuinely hard to categorize, one that is obviously spam, and one that looks urgent at first glance but is not. Vary the length from two lines to a couple of paragraphs. Use invented names and companies only.\n\nList the files when you are done, and show me one of them so I can see the shape of it.</pre>\n\n<h4>Step 4 · Teach it to read one message</h4>\n<p>Here is the central idea of the week. You are not asking for prose, you are asking for a fixed set of fields. That is what structured output means, and it is the thing that lets a program act on the answer instead of a person reading it.</p>\n<pre>In the inbox-triage folder, create triage_one.py.\n\nIt takes a filename as a command line argument, reads that message, sends it to Claude, and gets back a structured result with exactly these fields: urgency (one of high, medium, low), category (one of outage, billing, sales, internal, vendor, spam, unclear), one_line_summary, suggested_next_action, owner (which team should pick it up), and confidence (a number from 0 to 1 for how sure the model is about its own answer).\n\nUse the Anthropic SDK structured output support so those fields are guaranteed, rather than parsing them back out of prose. Print the result as a readable block, not as raw JSON.\n\nThen run it on inbox/msg01.txt and explain to me, in plain English, what structured output just gave us that an ordinary chat reply would not have.</pre>\n\n<h4>Step 5 · Run it across the whole inbox</h4>\n<p>This is where it stops being a script and becomes a workflow: one command, twelve messages, one table. Notice what we are adding, though. It keeps going when a single message fails instead of dying halfway through, and that difference is most of what separates a demo from something you would actually leave running.</p>\n<pre>Create triage_all.py in the inbox-triage folder, building on what triage_one.py already does.\n\nIt should process every .txt file in the inbox folder, print a progress line as it goes, and write the results to triage_results.csv with one row per message.\n\nMake it survive a bad message: if one file fails, record the failure in its row, carry on with the rest, and report how many succeeded and how many failed at the end. Retry a failed call once before giving up on it. Also track and print the total elapsed time and the total input and output tokens used across the whole run.\n\nRun it, then show me the CSV and tell me which messages the model was least confident about.</pre>\n\n<h4>Step 6 · Add the quality gate, and produce your deliverable</h4>\n<p>An assistant that is right most of the time is only useful if you know which times. This step adds the piece that makes it trustworthy: anything the model was unsure about goes to a person instead of being quietly acted on.</p>\n<p>The run_report.md file this produces is the one you upload below to complete this card.</p>\n<pre>Extend triage_all.py in the inbox-triage folder with a quality gate.\n\nAny message with confidence below 0.75, or marked high urgency, goes into a human review queue rather than being treated as settled. Everything else is auto-cleared.\n\nThen have it write run_report.md summarizing the run: the total number of messages, how many were auto-cleared versus flagged for review, the breakdown by urgency and by category, the specific messages that need a human and why, any failures, the elapsed time, and the token usage. Write it for a manager who is never going to open the CSV.\n\nSave run_report.md to my Downloads folder as well, and tell me the exact filename. Then explain to me in plain English why a confidence threshold is the difference between a demo and something you would be willing to run unattended.</pre>\n\n<h4>Step 7 · Stretch: point it at your own work</h4>\n<p>Optional, and the bridge to the rest of the program. The four-stage shape you just built (read the input, get structured fields back, run it across everything, gate on quality) is the shape of most business workflows. This step plans yours. Pick your project from the dropdown above first.</p>\n<pre>I have just built a workflow tool that reads a folder of messages, returns structured fields for each one, runs across the whole folder, and routes anything low-confidence to a human.\n\nHelp me apply that same four-stage shape to {PROJECT}. Ask me what I actually do by hand today, then work out with me: what the input is and where it lives, what fields the structured output should contain, what the quality bar should be and what happens when something falls below it, and what the finished deliverable looks like.\n\nWrite the result to workflow-plan.md and save a copy to my Downloads folder. Keep it to one page, in plain English, and no code yet.</pre>",
  "questions": [],
  "reflection": ""
}
                     $json$::jsonb,
                     true
                   ),
  updated_at     = now()
WHERE id = '160f9041-3d74-44b7-b713-3862937336ec';

-- Session 7 contract now matches the card: guided lab for the first half,
-- projects + the first Story 000 run for the second.
UPDATE live_sessions
-- NOTE: live_sessions has created_at only, no updated_at column.
SET
  description = $s$Build Day. First half is a guided lab: set up your .env and Claude API key, then build a Python tool that triages a whole inbox, returns structured fields for every message, runs the batch without falling over, and routes anything it is unsure about to a human. Second half we move to your own projects and the first Story 000 run. Bring a laptop and an Anthropic API key.$s$
WHERE session_number = 7
  AND cohort_id = '1f1d86f4-6da5-4767-a250-cd8310570bea';
